      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best sealer for concrete countertops?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a kitchen where cooking actually happens, a film-forming sealer such as epoxy gives better practical results because it prevents acid etching that a penetrating sealer cannot. If you want the surface to look and feel like concrete and can accept etch marks as patina, penetrating is the right choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does epoxy make concrete countertops heat resistant?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No, the opposite. Concrete handles heat well on its own; an epoxy film over it often softens, blooms white or yellows well below the temperature of a pan straight off the hob. Manufacturers state maximum temperatures and they are frequently lower than people assume. Trivets become permanent with a film sealer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you scratch a penetrating sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "There is nothing to scratch, because no film sits on the surface. A dragged pan or a knife slip marks the concrete itself rather than tearing a coating, and those marks blend into a matte textured surface far better than scratches show on a glossy epoxy film."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will lemon juice damage a sealed concrete countertop?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On a penetrating sealer, yes, given time — the acid reaches the cement paste and dissolves a microscopic layer, leaving a permanently duller etched patch. On an intact film sealer, no, because the acid never contacts the concrete. This single difference decides the choice for most working kitchens."
          }
