          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you repair a damaged epoxy countertop sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually by redoing the whole surface. Patching leaves a visible edge and sheen difference because you are adding thickness locally. Proper repair means abrading or stripping and recoating the entire countertop, which next to fitted cabinetry with limited ventilation is considerably harder than it sounds."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often do you reapply a penetrating countertop sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Typically every one to three years depending on use, and reapplication is trivial — clean and apply more, and it blends invisibly because there is no film edge. Many people also use a sacrificial paste wax over it, reapplied every few months, which takes the daily abrasion."
          }
