    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#core-trade" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Core Trade</a>
          <a href="#epoxy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Epoxy Gives You</a>
          <a href="#penetrating" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. What Penetrating Gives You</a>
          <a href="#acid" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Acid Problem</a>
          <a href="#heat-scratch" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Heat and Scratches</a>
          <a href="#repair" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Repairability</a>
          <a href="#choosing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Choosing for Your Kitchen</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Neither is better. They fail in opposite directions, and which failure you can live with depends on how the kitchen is used and how much you mind the surface looking like poured concrete rather than a finished worktop.
        </p>

        <div id="core-trade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Core Trade
          </h2>

          <p>
            A penetrating sealer soaks into the concrete and lines the pore structure from within. Nothing sits on the surface, so the concrete looks and feels exactly like concrete, and there is no film that can scratch, chip, peel or cloud. What it does is make the concrete repel water and oil so spills sit long enough to be wiped.
          </p>

          <p>
            An epoxy or other film-forming sealer cures into a continuous layer on top of the concrete. Spills never reach the concrete at all, so stain and acid resistance are far better. But there is now a layer that can be scratched, chipped, heat-marked and worn, and when it is damaged the repair is not local.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Penetrating</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Epoxy / Film</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Appearance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Natural concrete, matte</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coated, often glossy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Feel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smooth, plastic-like</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acid resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor — concrete is exposed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stain resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate — resists, not prevents</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heat resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Concrete's own, good</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often the weak point</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scratches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cannot — nothing to scratch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, and visible</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spot repair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easy — just reapply</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Difficult — usually redo</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Reapplication</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every 1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5+ years, then strip</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY friendliness</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to low</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read the acid row and the scratch row together, because they are the two failure modes and they are mutually exclusive. Choose penetrating and lemon juice will etch the concrete. Choose epoxy and a dragged pan will scratch the film. There is no product that avoids both, and anyone claiming otherwise is selling something.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HONEST FRAMING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pick the failure you can live with.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Penetrating fails as etch marks and stains in the concrete, which are permanent but blend into a surface that already looks like concrete. Epoxy fails as scratches and heat marks in a film, which are conspicuous precisely because the film is otherwise flawless. Both are real; neither is a defect.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="epoxy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Epoxy Gives You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy is the choice when protecting the concrete matters more than the concrete looking untouched.
          </p>

          <p>
            Genuine acid and stain resistance is the headline. A cured epoxy film is chemically resistant enough that wine, citrus, tomato, vinegar and coffee sit on the surface without reaching the concrete. For a kitchen where cooking actually happens and spills are not wiped within seconds, this is a substantial practical advantage.
          </p>

          <p>
            A finished, non-porous surface. Epoxy fills and levels, so pinholes and minor surface imperfections disappear under it, and the result is hygienic and easy to clean with no pore structure to hold anything. For food preparation that is a real benefit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Depth and gloss. Epoxy produces a wet, deep look that enhances the concrete colour considerably, which some people want and others specifically do not. It can be had in lower sheens but it will never look like bare concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weaknesses are equally concrete. Heat is the main one: many epoxies soften or mark well below the temperature of a pan straight off the hob, and a permanent white or yellowed mark is the usual result. Trivets stop being optional. Scratches show because the film is otherwise perfect. Some epoxies amber over time under UV, which matters near a sunny window. And application is less forgiving — mix ratios, pot life, bubbles and dust all matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Polyurethane sits alongside epoxy as the other common film option and is generally more heat and UV tolerant while being thinner and less chemically resistant. Many professional systems use an epoxy build coat with a polyurethane topcoat to get both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="penetrating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Penetrating Gives You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Penetrating is the choice when the material is the point and you want the surface to age rather than to be protected from ageing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The concrete stays concrete. No sheen change beyond a slight deepening, the texture is unchanged, and it feels like stone rather than like a coated surface. For anyone who chose a concrete countertop specifically because they wanted concrete, this is the whole argument and it is a good one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nothing to damage. There is no film, so there is nothing to scratch, chip, peel, cloud or delaminate. A dragged pan does nothing. A knife slip marks the concrete rather than tearing a coating. The surface cannot fail in the ways a film fails, which over a decade is worth something.
          </p>

          <p>
            Trivially repairable. When performance drops, you clean the surface and apply more sealer, and it blends invisibly because there is no film edge. Compare that to a damaged epoxy, where the repair is usually the whole surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weakness is one word: acid. A penetrating sealer reduces absorption but does not stop an acid reacting with the cement paste at the surface. Lemon juice, vinegar, wine and tomato left on the surface will etch a dull mark into the concrete, and etching is a physical change rather than a stain — it cannot be cleaned off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stain resistance is also relative rather than absolute. Oil in particular will darken concrete over time even through a good penetrating sealer if it is not wiped, and turmeric, beetroot and red wine can leave shadows. The sealer buys you minutes rather than immunity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many people mitigate this with a sacrificial wax over the penetrating sealer, reapplied every few months, which takes the abrasion and adds a modest barrier. It is a maintenance habit rather than a solution, and it suits people who enjoy that relationship with a material.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="acid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Acid Problem, Which Decides It for Most Kitchens
          </h2>

          <p>
            This deserves its own section because it is the single most consequential difference and the one people underestimate until it happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete is alkaline and cement paste reacts with acids. When lemon juice or vinegar sits on unprotected concrete, it dissolves a microscopic layer of the surface, leaving a duller patch. That is etching, and it is not a stain — there is nothing to clean off, because material has been removed.
          </p>

          <p>
            A film sealer prevents this completely while intact, because the acid never contacts the concrete. A penetrating sealer slows absorption but the acid still reaches the surface, so etching remains possible with prolonged contact.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much this matters depends entirely on the kitchen. A household that cooks with citrus, deglazes with wine, and does not wipe immediately will etch a penetrating-sealed countertop within a year. A household that uses boards for everything and wipes promptly may never see it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also an aesthetic argument that cuts the other way. Some people want a concrete countertop precisely because it records use — etch marks, patina, the evidence of a working kitchen. That is a legitimate position and it makes penetrating the right choice regardless of the technical comparison. What causes unhappiness is wanting a flawless surface and choosing the sealer that cannot deliver one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Decisive Question</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Acid</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">What penetrating cannot stop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Heat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">What epoxy handles worst</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Etch</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Material removed, not a stain</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Patina</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">The same thing, reframed</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="heat-scratch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Heat and Scratches
          </h2>

          <p>
            The mirror image, and the reason epoxy is not simply the better choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat tolerance is where film sealers are genuinely weak. Concrete itself handles heat well, but an epoxy film over it often does not, and a pan straight off the hob can leave a permanent white bloom, a yellowed patch or a softened depression. Manufacturers state maximum temperatures, and they are frequently well below what a cooking pan reaches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Polyurethane topcoats improve this meaningfully, which is why many professional systems use them over an epoxy build. But no film-sealed concrete countertop should be treated as heat-proof, and trivets are a permanent habit rather than a precaution.
          </p>

          <p>
            A penetrating-sealed countertop is much more forgiving here, because the heat meets concrete rather than a polymer. It is not indestructible — thermal shock can craze a surface and very hot pans can discolour — but the margin is far wider.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scratches follow the same logic. An epoxy film scratches, and the scratches are conspicuous because they interrupt an otherwise flawless glossy surface catching the light. Penetrating-sealed concrete can be marked but the mark sits in a textured matte surface that hides it far better.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, scratches on epoxy accumulate as a gradual dulling in the areas of heaviest use rather than as individual marks, which reads as wear rather than damage. Whether that bothers you is personal, and it is worth looking at a five-year-old epoxy countertop before committing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Repairability, Which Matters Over a Decade
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the factor that gets least attention at purchase and most attention five years later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers are reapplied, not repaired. Clean the surface, apply more sealer, and it absorbs and blends with what is already there. There is no edge, no overlap mark, and no need to treat the whole surface if only one area needs it. The whole process is an hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Film sealers cannot be spot-repaired convincingly. A patch of new epoxy over a damaged area leaves a visible edge and a sheen difference, because you are adding thickness in one place. Fixing a film properly means abrading or stripping the whole surface and recoating it, which on a fitted countertop is a serious job.
          </p>

          <p>
            That asymmetry compounds over time. A penetrating-sealed countertop at ten years has been topped up several times and looks as it always did. A film-sealed one has accumulated scratches and heat marks and eventually needs the full treatment, which on a kitchen in use is disruptive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stripping a film from a fitted countertop is also harder than stripping a floor or a patio: you cannot pressure wash it, the chemicals are working next to cabinetry and appliances, and ventilation in a kitchen is limited. Factor this into the decision rather than discovering it later.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical mitigation for film systems: keep a record of exactly which product was used and, if possible, some of it. Recoating within the same system is far more likely to succeed than guessing at compatibility years later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing for Your Kitchen
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match it to how the surface will actually be used, rather than to which sounds more protective.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">If this describes you</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Choose</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cook with citrus, wine, tomato daily</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy or film</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Want the concrete to look like concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hate visible scratches on a glossy surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Want a hygienic, non-porous food surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy or film</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Put hot pans down without thinking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Happy to maintain with wax every few months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Want to forget about it for five years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy or film</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bathroom vanity rather than kitchen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either; acid risk is low</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The row that decides it most often is the first against the third. A kitchen that cooks seriously wants acid protection; a household that cannot tolerate visible scratching wants no film. If both apply, the honest answer is that a concrete countertop may be the wrong material and a quartz or granite surface delivers both.
          </p>

          <p>
            A bathroom vanity is a genuinely easier case. Almost no acid exposure, no heat, gentler use — a penetrating sealer performs well there and keeps the material honest, and the main threats are toothpaste and cosmetics rather than lemon juice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hybrid approaches exist and are worth knowing about. A densifier followed by a penetrating sealer and a sacrificial wax gives meaningfully better performance than a penetrating sealer alone while keeping the natural look. And a professional epoxy build with a polyurethane topcoat addresses epoxy's heat and UV weaknesses. Our guide on sealing a concrete countertop covers the application sequence for both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, check the food-contact credentials of the specific product if food will touch the surface directly, which our guide on food-safe countertop sealers covers, and note the product and date somewhere durable so the next reapplication is straightforward.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Either One to Do Everything</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating cannot stop acid etching the concrete; epoxy cannot survive a pan straight off the hob. These are opposite failure modes and no product avoids both. Choosing well means deciding which failure you can live with, not finding one that has neither.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Penetrating for a Kitchen That Cooks With Citrus</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acid removes a microscopic layer of cement paste, leaving a permanently duller patch. That is etching rather than staining, so there is nothing to clean off. A household that deglazes with wine and cuts lemons on the counter will etch a penetrating-sealed surface within a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a Film-Sealed Countertop as Heat-Proof</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete handles heat well; the epoxy over it frequently does not, and manufacturers' maximum temperatures are often well below what a cooking pan reaches. A hot pan leaves a permanent white bloom or softened depression. Trivets are a permanent habit with a film sealer, not a precaution.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a Film Can Be Spot-Repaired</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Patching epoxy leaves a visible edge and a sheen difference because you are adding thickness in one place. Fixing a film properly means abrading or stripping the whole surface, which on a fitted countertop next to cabinetry with limited ventilation is a serious job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Recording Which Product Was Used</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Recoating within the same system is far more likely to succeed than guessing at compatibility years later, and this matters much more for film sealers than penetrating ones. Write the product and date somewhere durable, and keep some of the product if you can.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sealer for concrete countertops?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a kitchen where cooking actually happens, a film-forming sealer such as epoxy gives better practical results because it prevents acid etching that a penetrating sealer cannot. If you want the surface to look and feel like concrete and can accept etch marks as patina, penetrating is the right choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does epoxy make concrete countertops heat resistant?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, the opposite. Concrete handles heat well on its own; an epoxy film over it often softens, blooms white or yellows well below the temperature of a pan straight off the hob. Manufacturers state maximum temperatures and they are frequently lower than people assume. Trivets become permanent with a film sealer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you scratch a penetrating sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is nothing to scratch, because no film sits on the surface. A dragged pan or a knife slip marks the concrete itself rather than tearing a coating, and those marks blend into a matte textured surface far better than scratches show on a glossy epoxy film.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will lemon juice damage a sealed concrete countertop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a penetrating sealer, yes, given time — the acid reaches the cement paste and dissolves a microscopic layer, leaving a permanently duller etched patch. On an intact film sealer, no, because the acid never contacts the concrete. This single difference decides the choice for most working kitchens.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you repair a damaged epoxy countertop sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually by redoing the whole surface. Patching leaves a visible edge and sheen difference because you are adding thickness locally. Proper repair means abrading or stripping and recoating the entire countertop, which next to fitted cabinetry with limited ventilation is considerably harder than it sounds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do you reapply a penetrating countertop sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically every one to three years depending on use, and reapplication is trivial — clean and apply more, and it blends invisibly because there is no film edge. Many people also use a sacrificial paste wax over it, reapplied every few months, which takes the daily abrasion.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is epoxy or penetrating better for a bathroom vanity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating, usually. A vanity sees almost no acid exposure and no heat, so the main weakness of a penetrating sealer barely applies, while its natural look, scratch immunity and easy reapplication all remain advantages. The threats there are toothpaste and cosmetics rather than lemon juice.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

