        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The two fail in opposite directions and no product avoids both. A penetrating sealer leaves the concrete exposed to acid, so lemon juice and wine etch permanent dull patches into it. An epoxy film prevents that entirely and is itself vulnerable to heat and scratching, and cannot be spot-repaired when it is damaged.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            So choose by how the kitchen is used. Serious cooking with citrus, wine and tomato points at a film. Wanting concrete to look and feel like concrete, tolerating patina, and valuing a repair that takes an hour rather than a weekend points at penetrating. If you want a flawless surface that shrugs off both acid and hot pans, the honest answer is that concrete may be the wrong material.
          </p>
